Checked vs. Unchecked Exceptions

Java has checked and unchecked exceptions

checked exception
	An exception the programmer is expected to handle within the program
	If a method includes code that may throw a checked exception, the Java compiler ensures that the method catches the exception or declares that the exception is thrown.
	Ex: A program that opens files should anticipate and handle a FileNotFoundException.
	
unchecking exception
	An exception the programmer is not expected to catch or handle at runtime
	Compiler does nothing to verify unchecked exceptions are handled
	Ex: A program should try to eliminate code that uses null references instead of catching and

